{
  "chalk": 1,
  "name": "02-church-numerals.chalk",
  "cells": [
    {
      "src": "Church numerals and booleans",
      "type": "section",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "> [!goal]\n> Represent numbers and truth values as functions, and compute with them by reduction alone.",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "> [!definition] Church numerals\n> The number $n$ is the function that applies $f$ to $x$ $n$ times: $0 = \\lambda f.\\, \\lambda x.\\, x$, $1 = \\lambda f.\\, \\lambda x.\\, f\\ x$, $2 = \\lambda f.\\, \\lambda x.\\, f\\,(f\\ x)$, and so on.",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "The engine knows the numerals and a small library (`succ`, `add`, `mul`, `pow`, `true`, `false`, `not`, `and`, `or`, `if`, `iszero`, `pair`, `fst`, `snd`). It unfolds the names first, in one δ-step, then reduces, and reads a numeral back when the result is one.",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "succ 2",
      "showWork": true,
      "label": null,
      "stepwise": 0
    },
    {
      "src": "add 2 3",
      "showWork": true,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "mul 2 3",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "Your own definitions",
      "type": "section",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "`name := term` defines a term for the rest of the notebook.",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "twice := λf. λx. f (f x)",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "twice twice g z",
      "showWork": true,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "Booleans",
      "type": "section",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "> [!definition] Church booleans\n> $\\mathsf{true} = \\lambda t.\\, \\lambda f.\\, t$ and $\\mathsf{false} = \\lambda t.\\, \\lambda f.\\, f$: a boolean chooses one of two things. Then $\\mathsf{if}\\ b\\ x\\ y$ is just $b\\ x\\ y$.",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "and true false",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "if true a b",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "succ 1",
      "type": "exercise",
      "prompt": "Reduce $\\mathsf{succ}\\ 1$. Write the numeral out as a λ-term (or by its name).",
      "hints": [
        "$\\mathsf{succ}$ adds one more application of $f$."
      ],
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "mul 2 2",
      "type": "exercise",
      "prompt": "Reduce $\\mathsf{mul}\\ 2\\ 2$ to a numeral.",
      "hints": [
        "$2 \\cdot 2 = 4$: four applications of $f$."
      ],
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "not true",
      "type": "exercise",
      "prompt": "Reduce $\\mathsf{not}\\ \\mathsf{true}$.",
      "hints": [
        "The answer is a boolean: which one chooses its second argument?"
      ],
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "> [!summary]\n> With nothing but functions and substitution you get numbers, arithmetic and logic: the λ-calculus computes everything a computer can.",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    }
  ],
  "scenes": []
}
